markdown <<MARKDOWN
# Handling Uncertainty

Lesson 2 opens by ranking three events by likelihood: guessing a 4-digit passcode on the first try, your favorite song playing first from a shuffled 100-song playlist, and sharing a birthday with a new friend.

The real answer, ranked most to least likely: favorite song playing first (1 in 100), sharing a birthday (about 1 in 365), guessing the passcode (1 in 9,999). The strong-answer pattern is specific: compare the *number of possibilities*, not gut instinct, fewer possibilities means a higher chance any one of them happens.

<div style="background-color: #F7F7FB; border-left: 4px solid #4C42CF; border-radius: 8px; padding: 12px 16px; margin: 16px 0;">
  <div style="font-size: 16px; line-height: 1.5;"><strong>&#128214; Probability:</strong> a way to measure how likely something is to happen.</div>
</div>

Probability runs from 0 to 1. Rolling a die: probability of landing on 1-6 is 1 (certain), probability of rolling a 7 is 0 (impossible), probability of rolling even is 0.5 (3 of 6 outcomes). To calculate it: outcomes for the event, divided by total possible outcomes.

<div style="background-color: #F7F7FB; border-left: 4px solid #4C42CF; border-radius: 8px; padding: 12px 16px; margin: 16px 0;">
  <div style="font-size: 16px; line-height: 1.5;"><strong>&#128214; Module:</strong> a Python file that can be imported into other Python files.</div>
</div>

<div style="border-radius: 10px; overflow: hidden; margin: 12px 0 16px 0; border: 1px solid #E4E2F8;">
  <div style="background-color: #1F1976; padding: 16px 20px; font-family: 'Consolas', 'Courier New', monospace; font-size: 16px; color: #E4E2F8; line-height: 1.8;">
import random<br>
print(random.randint(1, 10))
  </div>
  <div style="background-color: #E4E2F8; padding: 10px 20px; font-family: 'Consolas', 'Courier New', monospace; font-size: 16px; color: #1F1976;">
8
  </div>
</div>

<code style="font-size:16px;">random</code> is Python's built-in module for randomness. <code style="font-size:16px;">random.randint(1, 10)</code> picks a random whole number from 1 to 10, inclusive.

<div style="background-color: #E4E2F8; border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px 20px; margin: 16px 0;">
  <div style="font-size: 16px; color: #222222; line-height: 1.5;"><strong>&#128161; Teacher tip:</strong> students apply this in Simulating a Party (Lesson 2, Levels 1-3), simulating a pizza order of 42 slices against randomly generated requests, before a Check for Understanding on Level 4.</div>
</div>
MARKDOWN
